Certainly! Here's a JavaScript program that utilizes advanced features such as async/await, Promises, classes, and destructuring:

```javascript
class DataFetcher {
    constructor(url) {
        this.url = url;
    }

    async fetchData() {
        const response = await fetch(this.url);
        const data = await response.json();
        return data;
    }
}

class DataProcessor {
    static process(data) {
        return data.map(({ id, title }) => ({ id, title }));
    }
}

(async () => {
    const apiURL = 'https: 
    const fetcher = new DataFetcher(apiURL);

    try {
        const data = await fetcher.fetchData();
        const processedData = DataProcessor.process(data);

        processedData.forEach(({ id, title }) => {
            console.log(`Post ${id}: ${title}`);
        });
    } catch (error) {
        console.error('Error fetching data:', error);
    }
})();
```

This program fetches data from a public API, processes it, and logs a simplified version of each post's ID and title to the console.